{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And the shoham stone.</b> White, and in Aramaic burla, and in the Ishmaelite tongue balawr [beryl?], and we have no way to ascertain the stones of the ephod, for the Gaon [Saadia] translated them at his discretion, for he has no tradition to rely upon, for behold we have seen that he translated sappir as white, when it is only red, and there is no argument from odem written first, for it is known that there are five colors of the eye: white and berekhet [emerald?], red, green, and black, and since red is the middle one it is found in many kinds. And he translated nophekh as a blackish hue on account of 'and put her eyes in your mouth' (2 Kings 9:30), and this is impossible, for the nun of nophekh cannot but be a root letter, for it is on the pattern of odem. And a great sage of Spain said that the yahalom is the one called almam, which breaks all stones, and it bores the bedolach [crystal] from the root 'he strikes' (Isaiah 41:7), and the yod is added like the yod of yitzhar; likewise ahlamah, el halamah, and it is a stone such that whoever has that stone on his finger will always see dreams. And one should not wonder at this, for a power is found in the nature of every single stone, for there is a stone that draws iron, and one that stays the blood, and one that flees from vinegar, and one that is always broken upon a triangle."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {0 "The phrase 'the stones of the ephod' translates אבני המלואים, which literally means 'the stones of filling/setting'; Ibn Ezra appears to use the term loosely. 'Berekhet' and 'almam' are left transliterated as they are technical terms whose precise gem identity is debated. The word rendered 'stays' (מעמדת) could also mean 'coagulates.'"}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:38:55.592991409Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}